## Layer 10 — Moonshine address pipeline & the separation theorem (`MonsterAddress.lean`, `MoonshineAddress.lean`)

The full three-stage pipeline that gives every hashable object a coordinate in
the Monster's moonshine lattice, with proofs that distinct objects land in
distinct locations.

* **`RequestProject/MonsterAddress.lean`** — the 15-prime address with a genuine
  Chinese-Remainder-Theorem separation: `addr_inj` proves the 15-residue address
  is injective on `[0, radical)` (`radical = 2·3·…·71`), `location_inj` lifts this
  to injectivity of the assembled `embed : ℕ → Fin 194 → Fin 104 → ℤ` moonshine
  point, and `objects_locations_distinct` verifies a concrete sample list lands at
  pairwise-distinct locations.
* **`RequestProject/MoonshineAddress.lean`** — the T₁ (untwisted j) pipeline:
  * Stage 1 `primeAddress h j = h % mp j` (the ℤ¹⁵ address over the 15 Monster
    primes);
  * Stage 2 `irrepWeight h i = jCoeff (i%15+2) · (h % mp (i%15))` — a diagonal
    projection into the 194 irreps, each tuned to one Monster prime;
  * Stage 3 `moonshineCoord h i k = irrepWeight h i · jCoeff (k+2)` into
    ℤ^(194×104) = ℤ^20176 (`moonshine_dimension`);
  * `separation_local` / `separation` — distinct prime residues separate objects
    in moonshine space; `threadLabel` / `tgIndex` scaffold the future 170
    McKay–Thompson `T_g` threads (the 194-stranded braid).

## Layer 11 — FRACTRAN walk across the irrep biomes (`FractranWalk.lean`)

Lifts a concrete computational process into the moonshine coordinate system:
`fractranLift P start t = moonshinePoint (fractranState P start t)` sends each
step of a FRACTRAN program to its point in ℤ^(194×104).  The worked example
`mulProgram = [(3,2)]` started at `8 = 2³` walks `8 → 12 → 18 → 27` across four
distinct irrep biomes (`mul_trajectory`, `mul_visits_four_biomes`,
`mul_walks_biomes`), and `mul_moves_in_moonshine` proves its moonshine coordinate
genuinely changes on the first step.

## Layer 12 — PRIMEGAME through the Monster biomes (`FractranBiome.lean`, `FractranBiomeSVG.lean`)

Runs the real thing: **Conway's prime-generating PRIMEGAME** (the original
fourteen-fraction FRACTRAN program) traced step-by-step through the
`MoonshineAddress` pipeline.  Each integer state `n` is sent to its point
`moonshinePoint n ∈ ℤ^(194×104)`, so the whole computation is a path in moonshine
space (`primegamePath`), and each state lights up its biome (primary irrep
`n % 194`).

`FractranBiome.lean` proves the trace: `primegame_step0` (`2 → 15`),
`primegame_step19` (step 19 returns `4 = 2²`, the prime 2), `primegame_traj20`
and `primegame_biomes20` (the explicit 20-step state/biome traces),
`primegame_biomes_visited` (18 distinct biomes), `primegame_moves_in_moonshine`
/ `trajectory_distinct_steps_0_1` (the lift genuinely moves), and
`primegame_pow2_biome` (every prime-witnessing pure power of two sits at residue
0 of the Monster prime 2).  The structural highlight is
`primegame_monster_native`: **every numerator and denominator of all fourteen
fractions is Monster-prime-smooth**, so PRIMEGAME is natively typed for the
moonshine address system.

`FractranBiomeSVG.lean` visualises the lift exactly as requested — *on the
q-expansion from 1–104*. The redesign pins one variable: the **entire PRIMEGAME
path is drawn at once** as a single continuous curve through the 194-biome ring
(the elliptic-curve-like trajectory), and the **q-expansion is mapped along that
fixed curve as colour** — each segment is coloured by the hue of its j-coefficient
(`coloredPath`, `hueOfPage`). The only moving element is a single q-expansion
*cursor* (`walker`) that glides along the already-drawn curve, flaring gold on
every pure power of two (the prime-generating events). So the fractran path no
longer moves; only the q-expansion does, swept along the static curve. Commands
`#fractranbiome` and `#writefractranbiome "path"` regenerate the artifact
`atlas_fractran.svg` (well-formed, self-playing animated SVG) at build time.

## Layer 13 — Irrep fold: decls squeezed into the available primes (`IrrepFold.lean`)

Forces every declaration into the available prime dimensions, fold by fold, so we
watch the whole source code **deform** while the dependency **arrows are
preserved**.  Nothing is ever placed outside Monster-prime space
(`foldLayers_subset`).

* **The folds.** A *layer* is the list of primes that exist in that step
  (`foldLayers`).  Step 1 is the empty layer — every decl collapses to **one
  point** on the surface of the sphere; step 2 is the requested `71·59·47`
  (`foldLayers_one`); step 3 is `71·59·41·31` (`foldLayers_two`); the remaining
  steps grow through prefixes of the fifteen Monster primes, ending at the full
  faithful fifteen-prime address.

* **Folding.** `foldPos h ps` squeezes the atom with hash `h` onto exactly the
  dimensions of layer `ps`: each available prime `pᵢ` contributes its residue
  fraction `(h mod pᵢ)/pᵢ` along direction `2π i / n`, and the normalised vector
  sum is the location.  The empty layer has no dimensions, hence the single
  shared point.

* **Arrows preserved.** Each declaration's outgoing references (extracted via
  `Expr.getUsedConstants` over its value and type, `projectFoldAtoms` /
  `foldEdges`) become arrows.  Every atom carries one SMIL `translate` animation
  that morphs it continuously through its per-layer locations, and every arrow is
  a line whose four endpoint coordinates are animated **in lockstep** with the
  two atoms it joins — so the arrows stretch and bend through every fold but
  never detach.

Commands `#irrepfold` and `#writeirrepfold "path"` regenerate the artifact
`atlas_irrepfold.svg` (well-formed, self-playing animated SVG; 64 decls, 65
arrows, 14 folds) at build time.

### Layer: Irrep194 (`RequestProject/Irrep194.lean`)

Two more animated SVGs, plus the formalisation of the compaction observed in
`atlas_irrepfold.svg`.

* **194-step irrep walk** (`atlas_irrep194.svg`).  One frame per Monster irrep
  (`numIrrepSteps = Moonshine.numIrreps = 194`; `stepIrrep_surjective` shows the
  walk visits every irrep exactly once).  Each declaration sits at the angle of
  its primary irrep `h % 194`, and the whole constellation contracts toward the
  centre as the *compaction radius* shrinks.  The observation that the earlier
  fold "got compacter and compacter over time" is now a proved law:
  `compactionRadius k = outerR·compBase/(compBase+k)` is monotone non-increasing
  (`compaction_antitone`, never re-expands) and strictly shrinks `400 → 68`
  across the walk (`compaction_strict`).  A dashed outline ring animates through
  exactly these radii, and the dependency arrows are preserved in lockstep.
  Commands: `#irrep194`, `#writeirrep194 "path"`.

* **T1–T170 Thompson views sampled in 104 expansions** (`atlas_thompson.svg`).
  Each declaration carries a Thompson class `thompsonOf h = h % 170`
  (`thompsonOf_lt`); each of the 104 q-expansion pages samples one view
  `thompsonView pi`, keyed by `[q^{pi+1}] j mod 170` (`thompsonView_lt`).  Turning
  the 104 pages lights up the atoms in the sampled view.  Finding
  (`thompson_sample_count`): the 104 expansions sample exactly **49** of the 170
  views.  Commands: `#thompson`, `#writethompson "path"`.

* **Precomputed T1 (= j) expansion and its irrep configurations**
  (`RequestProject/JExpansion.lean`).  The supplied list of the first **100**
  nontrivial coefficients of `T1 = j - 744` is recorded as `t1Coeffs`, and
  `t1Coeffs_correct` proves it equals the honestly computed power series exactly
  (`t1Coeffs[k] = jSeries[k+2]`, the coefficient of `q^{k+1}` in `j`), so the
  precomputed data is verified rather than assumed (`t1Coeffs_eq_jSeries` is the
  pointwise form).  The moonshine "configurations" — each coefficient as a
  non-negative integer combination of the smallest Monster irrep degrees — are
  extended from `moonshine_c1 … c3` to `moonshine_c4` (multiplicities `(3,3,1,2,1)`)
  and `moonshine_c5` (`(4,5,3,2,1,1,1)`).
* `RequestProject/MonsterReach.lean` — how far the `j`-expansion has to run before a
  single coefficient `c(n)` first exceeds the order of the Monster
  `Holograms.monsterOrder = |𝕄|`.  The answer is exactly four coefficients beyond
  the recorded 100: every coefficient through `q¹⁰³` is below `|𝕄|`
  (`jCoeff_below_monster_through_103`, and `t1Coeffs_below_monster` for the recorded
  table), `c(104)` is the first to exceed it (`jCoeff_104_exceeds_monster`), and
  `c(105)` is over as well (`jCoeff_105_exceeds_monster`).  `monster_size_crossover_at_104`
  packages the crossover: `c(n) < |𝕄|` for all `n ≤ 103` and `|𝕄| < c(104)`.  All
  checked by kernel computation against the honest `jSeries`.
* `RequestProject/SpectralAtlas.lean` — the **multihash / graded** refinement of the
  moonshine projection.  Instead of one scalar hash, an object is a *multihash*
  `μ : Fin 15 → ℕ` (one channel per Monster prime), and each of the 194 irreps is a
  distinct lens reading its own channel (`irrepWeightV`).  The q-expansion composes
  these into **graded jets** `gradedPoint μ N` (the embedding truncated to the first
  `N` q-pages).  Proved properties: `gradedPoint_prefix` (higher grades restrict to
  lower ones — a compatible tower of finer approximations), `fullPoint_scalar` (on a
  constant multihash the pipeline reduces exactly to `MoonshineAddress.moonshinePoint`,
  so this strictly generalises the scalar atlas), `grade_zero_uninformative` (the
  grade-0 jet is identical for every object — maximal privacy),
  `grade_zero_collision_separated` (objects can share the grade-0 jet yet separate
  once a single q-page is revealed — a lossy→faithful hierarchy), and
  `spectral_separation_local` / `spectral_separation` (multihashes differing in their
  prime channels land at distinct coordinates).  The AST-driven multihash
  `langMultiHash` packages the 12 `Expr` constructor counts plus `size`, `depth`,
  `consts` (exactly 15 invariants) so that syntactic structure — not a single opaque
  hash — drives the embedding; `#spectral Nat.add` prints this address.

## Layer — `QDeform.lean` (the projection deformed along the 104 q-expansions)

`atlas_qdeform.svg` deforms the whole projection along the 104 q-expansions and
shows what happens as the number of available primes changes with each irrep
combination.  For page `n` the **primes available** are exactly the Monster primes
dividing the coefficient `[q^n] j` — the prime support of that page's irrep
combination (`availLayer`, `availCount`).  Every project declaration is folded
onto exactly those available primes (`qPos`, reusing `IrrepFold.foldPos`), so as
the pages turn the projection morphs continuously; the dependency **arrows are
preserved** (`IrrepFold.foldEdges`, endpoints animated in lockstep), and a live
inset graph plots `availCount` across the 104 pages with a cursor on the current
page.  Findings (all `native_decide`): `qdeform_max_count` (the count never
exceeds **6**), `qdeform_collapse` (page 71 / `q^71` is coprime to every Monster
prime, so the projection collapses to one point — `availLayer 70 = []`),
`qdeform_peak` (page 95 hits the maximum 6), `qdeform_distinct_counts` (exactly
**7** distinct counts, the values `0 … 6`), and `availLayer_subset` (nothing ever
leaves Monster-prime space).  Commands: `#qdeform`, `#writeqdeform "path"`.

## Layer — `DualAtlasSVG.lean` (the dual-atlas functor, as an animation)

`RequestProject/DualAtlasSVG.lean` turns the `DualAtlas` construction into a
single self-contained, self-playing **animated SVG**.  Everything it draws is
computed natively by Lean from the same definitions the proofs use
(`primalAtlas`/`dualAtlas` via `Holograms.dualizePair`, `cogProj`, `triadProj`,
