## Breaking the persistent smushes with the q-expansion (`RequestProject/QExpansionLadder.lean`)

The resolution ladder leaves a residue of **persistent (semantic) smushes** —
irrep-1 collisions that survive the full fifteen-prime address `fullModulus =
2·3·5·…·71`. This module attacks them with the McKay–Thompson `T1 = j`
q-expansion `c(1), c(2), …`, measuring up to **100 q-expansions**, in two layers:

* **Layer 1 (theorem `monster_q_cannot_break`).** Grading by which *Monster*
  primes divide each `c(n)` is provably hopeless: the radical of those primes
  divides `fullModulus`, so by `coarsen` a persistent smush automatically agrees
  modulo it. No Monster-prime q-content ever separates a persistent smush.
* **Layer 2 (the full coefficients).** Using the whole integer `c(n)` as a fresh
  coordinate adds non-Monster primes and unbounded size. Since `2^64 ≤ c(14)`
  (`hashRange_le_qCoeff14`), any two *distinct* hashes are broken by depth 14
  (`distinct_hashes_break_by_14`), and a smush survives all 100 q-expansions iff
  its members carry the **literally identical** hash (`unbreakable_iff_equal_hash`).

**Result on the 120,000-declaration sample:** of the 1,969 persistent smush
groups, **0** are broken at any depth 1…100 and **1,969 are unbreakable** —
every persistent smush is a group of declarations whose statement fingerprints
are literally equal, so there is nothing for any q-expansion to separate. The
per-depth curve is written to `atlas_qexpansion.json`. Commands `#qladder` (full
corpus) and `#qladder_sample n` regenerate it.

## The persistent smushes as a glossary of duplicates (`RequestProject/DuplicateGlossary.lean`)

Since the persistent smushes are exactly the declarations whose statement
fingerprints are *literally identical*, this module reframes them from noise into
signal: a **glossary of duplicates**. It groups every declaration by its statement
fingerprint `info.type.hash` and, for each group of ≥ 2 members, writes a glossary
entry pairing two faces:

* the **syntax face** — how the members diverge in *name / namespace / spelling*
  despite the shared fingerprint, with the pretty-printed statement(s) and whether
  those strings are byte-identical (`syntaxIdentical`) or only hash-identical (a
  genuine *syntax delta*, e.g. `cngen` vs `cNGen`). It also records whether the
  member types are structurally `Expr`-equal, so a rare hash-only collision of
  truly different statements is flagged rather than silently asserted equal.
* the **proof-semantics face** — *how* the duplicate arose, by inspecting the
  stored proof terms: `alias` (all members carry the literally identical proof
  term), `rfl-variant` (some member's proof is a reflexivity/triviality term),
  `independent` (all members have proof terms, but distinct ones), `deprecated`
  (some member is tagged `@[deprecated]`), or `unknown` (some member exposes no
  proof value). Priority when several apply: `deprecated` > `alias` >
  `rfl-variant` > `independent` > `unknown`.

Grouping by the exact fingerprint is finer than the resolution ladder's
base-cell tally (a single irrep-1 cell can host several distinct-hash duplicate
groups, in which case the ladder counts the cell as *geometric* and drops the
duplicates): on the 120,000-declaration sample the strict-fingerprint grouping
finds **3,436** duplicate groups — alias 1,671, independent 1,266, unknown 353,
deprecated 124, rfl-variant 22; 2,938 with byte-identical statements and 498 with
a syntax delta (3 of those hash-only). The machine-readable glossary is written to
`atlas_duplicates.json` and the human-readable one to `DuplicateGlossary.md`.
Commands `#dupglossary` (full corpus) and `#dupglossary_sample n` regenerate both.

Because `atlas_duplicates.json` is ~7 MB and not meant to be read by eye, a small
set of viewable summaries is generated from it by `gen_embodiment.py`:

* `DuplicateReport/` — a 10-page digest (`page_01.md` … `page_10.md`, `index.md`):
  what a "duplicate" means, the five proof-semantics classes, 2–3 exemplars per
  class, the non-autogenerated (user-code) duplicates, and the hash-only collisions.
* `EmbodimentReport.md` / `atlas_embodiment.json` — 2–3 exemplars per class with
  namespace splits, deprecated/rfl flags, and proof-term heads.
* `atlas_user_duplicates.json` — only the duplicate groups whose members are all
  hand-written (no Lean-generated `match_`/`_simp_`/`_private`/`.rec`/… names);
  **560** such groups (independent 344, unknown 113, deprecated 91, alias 11,
  rfl-variant 1).

Regenerate the summaries from the atlas with `python3 gen_embodiment.py`.

