## Fiber homes — the natural home of every declaration (`RequestProject/FiberHome.lean`)

This layer reads the three dual-atlas coordinates as a **fiber bundle** and gives
every declaration in the whole environment a single *natural home* cell:

* the **base** is the `92 × 20` cognitive/semantic grid (`cog` column × `triad`
  row), `1840` base cells;
* the **fibre** over each base point is the `5`-element harmonic class
  (trivector / Muse / heptad / hemispheres / recursion), normalised to a rank
  `0..4` by `harmRank`;
* the total space has `92 · 20 · 5 = 9200` cells, and `homeIndex` linearises a
  cell to an integer in `[0, 9200)`.

Verified fiber-address algebra (standard axioms only):

* `homeIndex_lt` — every declaration lands in `[0, 9200)`;
* `homeIndex_determines_cell` — the linear address determines all three
  coordinates (the mixed-radix linearisation is injective on its range);
* `SimilarHome` ("lands in the same cell") is an `Equivalence`
  (`similarHome_equiv`) and is exactly cell-equality (`similarHome_iff_cell`) —
  this is the formal meaning of "find similar declarations".

Reflection commands (run natively over the entire loaded environment):

* `#findhome f` — the natural home of `f` plus the population of that home cell;
* `#similar f` — list the declarations sharing `f`'s home cell (its nearest
  neighbours); writes `atlas_fiberhome.json`;
* `#fibercensus` — occupancy of the bundle (≈ 707k declarations land in ≈ 5.5k
  of the 9200 cells; busiest cell ≈ 2.6k declarations);
* `#fiberatlas` / `#writefibers "path"` — render the whole corpus as a heat-map
  over the `92 × 20` base grid, each base cell coloured by its dominant harmonic
  fibre and shaded by population. The generated picture is `atlas_fibers.svg`.

